<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.1.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01747</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.1.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>26efa948-7f15-4be2-b8c7-b24942af0903</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_8f7cc46d-a1c4-4f5a-a354-15049b2a8deb" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Here are a few pairs of positive numbers whose sum is 50.
       </para>
      <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
        <tgroup cols="4"><tbody><row>
            <entry><emphasis>Row</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>First number</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Second number</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Product</emphasis></entry></row>
        <row>
            <entry>a</entry>
            <entry>1</entry>
            <entry>49</entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>b</entry>
            <entry>2</entry>
            <entry>48</entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>c</entry>
            <entry>10</entry>
            <entry>40</entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      
      <para>Find the product in Row a.
         </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! 1 times 49 is 49.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.1.2 - 7.1.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 49.
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_0728daae-21c0-401c-b410-cb252946effe" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the product in Row b.
         </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! 2 times 48 is 96.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.1.2 – 7.1.3. 
     </para><para>The correct answer is 96.
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="afe95dd3-a516-43ec-b210-ee6bd60ec0ed" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the product in Row c.
         </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! 10 times 40 is 400.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.1.2 – 7.1.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 400.
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_9413357b-ef26-4b49-81e1-b738b2a4b131" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Use the following table to answer questions 2 – 5.</para><para>Here are some lengths and widths of a rectangle whose perimeter is 20 meters.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
        <tgroup cols="4"><tbody><row>
            <entry><emphasis>Row</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Length (meters)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Width (meters)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Area (square meters)</emphasis></entry></row>
        <row>
            <entry>a</entry>
            <entry>1</entry>
            <entry>9</entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>b</entry>
            <entry>3</entry>
            <entry>7</entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>c</entry>
            <entry>5</entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>d</entry>
            <entry>7</entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>e</entry>
            <entry>9</entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      
      <para>Complete the<emphasis>Width</emphasis> column of the table.</para>
      <para>Width in Row c

         </para>
    </problem><solution><para>Correct! If the length is 5, the width must be 5.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.1.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 5.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_3bdbe4a2-8ac9-4461-8e39-cab8b8c02048" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Width in Row d
         </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! If the length is 7, the width must be 3.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.1.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 3.
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_8c6e57ab-e5ec-4735-bb3c-e9296390b2f0" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Width in Row e
         </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! If the length is 9, the width is 1.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.1.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 1.
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="d3705748-965d-4519-81a4-22a359660d91" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Complete the<emphasis>Area</emphasis> column of the table. What do you notice about the areas?
       </para>
      <para>Area in Row a
         </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! 1 times 9 is 9.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.1.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 9.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="b3be8fc8-90dd-4020-afff-fecc37ee83c3" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Area in Row b
         </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! 3 times 7 is 21.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.1.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 21.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_28905c62-873f-4998-8834-2875d93e14bb" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Area in Row c
         </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! 5 times 5 is 25.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.1.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 25.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="c2123080-4bef-4b47-83bf-ad4a201a5385" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Area in Row d
         </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! 7 times 3 is 21.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.1.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 21.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_49e0db2c-26d0-4a5d-ae10-5644da180b14" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Area in Row e
         </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! 1 times 9 is 9.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.1.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 9.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="d02a5393-96ee-4823-b3bd-575d5e8a35ef" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Without calculating, predict whether the area of the rectangle will be greater than or less than 25 square
          meters if the length is 5.25 meters.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! When the length is 5.25, the width will be 4.75, so it can be estimated that the product of those
        numbers will be less than 25.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.1.3.</para><para>The correct answer is less than.
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_2c91b4ba-3408-4fa1-ba80-b510c31a9a90" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Plot the points for length and area from your table. Do the values change in a linear way? Do they change in
          an exponential way?

       </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! As the length increases, the area increases then decreases.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.1.3 – 7.1.4 to test your understanding? Then come
        back and try this question again.</para><para>The correct answer is not linear and not exponential.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_0b7d1133-8e47-4d76-a04a-36a5d5a241c9" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Use the following to answer questions 6 – 8.</para><para>Copies of a book are arranged in a stack. Each copy of a book is 2.1 cm thick.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
        <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
            <entry><emphasis>Row</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Copies of book</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Stack height in cm</emphasis></entry></row>
        <row>
            <entry>a</entry>
            <entry>0</entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>b</entry>
            <entry>1</entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>c</entry>
            <entry>2</entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>d</entry>
            <entry>3</entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>e</entry>
            <entry>4</entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      
      <para>Complete the table.</para>
      <para>Stack height in Row a
         </para>
        
    </problem><solution><para>Correct! When there are 0 books, there is no stack height.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.1.4.</para><para>The correct answer is 0.

     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_38e1ff43-c26b-43fc-9560-947c7ee6ea56" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Stack height in Row b

         </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! One book has a height of 2.1 cm.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.1.4.</para><para>The correct answer is 2.1.
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_419a0986-7b9f-40e3-828e-2fbe9ee4043e" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Stack height in Row c
         </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! Two books have a stack height of 2.1 cm times 2.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.1.4.</para><para>The correct answer is 4.2.
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_4defe410-53c8-467c-b4cf-a2518bf97b3f" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Stack height in Row d

         </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! Three books have a stack height of 2.1 cm times 3.
     </para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.1.4. 
     </para><para>The correct answer is 6.3.
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_465ca85a-f0bd-4661-8ae6-478e3280142b" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Stack height in Row e
         </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! Four books have a stack height of 2.1 cm times 4.
     </para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.1.4.</para><para>The correct answer is 8.4.
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_90ba40e9-2d43-41d8-b4e6-cc9fc03e6d17" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the difference in the height of the stack of books when a new copy of the book is added?
       </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 2.1</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.1.4.</para><para>The correct answer is 2.1.
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="e2ebe3a0-0691-4779-920c-ad91d7934afc" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>How high is a stack of \(b\) books, in centimeters?
       </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\((2.1)b\)</para><para>\(2.1 +b\)</para><para>\(2.1(b-1)\)</para><para>\(2.1^b\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! Each book is 2.1 cm, so multiply that by the number of books in the stack.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.1.4.</para><para>The correct answer is \((2.1)b\).
     </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
